      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784696621945,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Search posts by keyword\n\n1. Bad Solution : Use RDBMS and SQL query\n- Keep all post in RDBMS and query like SELECT * FROM Posts LIKE '%$keyword%';\nProblem : Terribly slow, Need to perform a SCAN(visit all records) and run contains, even though we index it, still super slow as Petabytes of data\nSharding | replication improves a bit but still dramatically slow. N posts over M nodes, for a search we just check on N/M posts for each node\n\n\n2. Great Solution : Create an Inverted Index\n- Create a dictionary which maps keywords to documents that contain them. So a map of keywords to posts.\n- When searching grab the search key from the DB and return the associated posts with that key\n- Use Redis for this to return data in < 1ms, durability concerns but could be overcome with Redis high availability.\n- When posts are created we'll break them into individual keywords, dropping the common words(A, and, the etc) knows as tokenization and clenzing and then append the post Id to the list of keywords\nProblem : These list of PostId are going to be very large, need to address this, need to write to many keys for every single post, post might have 10-1k keywords.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Search posts by keyword\n\n1. Bad Solution : Use RDBMS and SQL query\n- Keep all post in RDBMS and query like SELECT * FROM Posts LIKE '%$keyword%';\nProblem : Terribly slow, Need to perform a SCAN(visit all records) and run contains, even though we index it, still super slow as Petabytes of data\nSharding | replication improves a bit but still dramatically slow. N posts over M nodes, for a search we just check on N/M posts for each node\n\n\n2. Great Solution : Create an Inverted Index\n- Create a dictionary which maps keywords to documents that contain them. So a map of keywords to posts.\n- When searching grab the search key from the DB and return the associated posts with that key\n- Use Redis for this to return data in < 1ms, durability concerns but could be overcome with Redis high availability.\n- When posts are created we'll break them into individual keywords, dropping the common words(A, and, the etc) knows as tokenization and clenzing and then append the post Id to the list of keywords\nProblem : These list of PostId are going to be very large, need to address this, need to write to many keys for every single post, post might have 10-1k keywords.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "l5xpJNLn03DAWAF4x2h52",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -69214.53085555893,
      "y": -7536.748266598308,
      "width": 1724.6390380859375,
      "height": 400,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a6",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 378735011,
      "version": 1514,
      "versionNonce": 1446320419,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784697592579,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Get search result sorted by recency or like count\n- When searched for a keyword, we need to return posts which are either sort by recency or sorted by likes.\n\n1. Bad Solution : Request-Time Sorting\n- Grab all the post Ids for a given keyword, lookup the TS or like count from LikesDB or PostsDB, then sort them in memory.\nProblem : List of postId will be very large and fetching data for all those posts will take a lot of time and then sorting them all in memory. ughh\n\n\n2. Great Solution : Multiple Indexes\n- Have two separate indexes in redis one sorted by creation time and one sorted by the like count(say creation index and likes index) \n- For creation index, use simple redis list and just append at the end and just take the last elements.\n- For likes index, use Redis sorted set. This allows us to keep list of items ordered by a score just like a PQ\n- When a new post is created we add it to both the indexes for every keyword it contains, when a like event happens we update the score in our sorted set for the likes index\nProblem : Doubled in storage by valid tradeoff against the query performance but costs more\nlikes are happening quite frequently, for each like update it will put a lot of stress on Redis - To be addressed later\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Get search result sorted by recency or like count\n- When searched for a keyword, we need to return posts which are either sort by recency or sorted by likes.\n\n1. Bad Solution : Request-Time Sorting\n- Grab all the post Ids for a given keyword, lookup the TS or like count from LikesDB or PostsDB, then sort them in memory.\nProblem : List of postId will be very large and fetching data for all those posts will take a lot of time and then sorting them all in memory. ughh\n\n\n2. Great Solution : Multiple Indexes\n- Have two separate indexes in redis one sorted by creation time and one sorted by the like count(say creation index and likes index) \n- For creation index, use simple redis list and just append at the end and just take the last elements.\n- For likes index, use Redis sorted set. This allows us to keep list of items ordered by a score just like a PQ\n- When a new post is created we add it to both the indexes for every keyword it contains, when a like event happens we update the score in our sorted set for the likes index\nProblem : Doubled in storage by valid tradeoff against the query performance but costs more\nlikes are happening quite frequently, for each like update it will put a lot of stress on Redis - To be addressed later\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
