    {
      "id": "YeBYBo0ksmvNOd8tf-DMv",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -66415.56050433527,
      "y": -9189.496036961731,
      "width": 1653.8592529296875,
      "height": 375,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a9",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 539984963,
      "version": 1007,
      "versionNonce": 2103316355,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784699502304,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to handle large vol. of requests from users?\n- We don't have personalisation so same cached result could be returned to many users\n- We have 1min before a post needs to appear in search result - eventual consistency\n\n1. Good Solution : Use distributed cache alongside our search service\n- Store most recent results for a given search query, whenever queried search cache first, if not present hit index db, and then populate cache and return result to user\n- Have TTL of < 1 minute to guarantee we're never serving results that might not contain newly created posts.\n\n\n2. Great Solution : Use a CDN at the edge\n- Use CloudFare or AWS CloudFront to serve search results quickly\n- Most CDNs operate like a big set of geographically-spread HTTP caches.\n- configure an \"origin\" or target for the cache and configure DNS to route through the CDN.\n- If CDN doesn't get a hit, it proxies the request back to our servers to handle\n- add cache-control headers which tell our CDN when and for how long to cache a result.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to handle large vol. of requests from users?\n- We don't have personalisation so same cached result could be returned to many users\n- We have 1min before a post needs to appear in search result - eventual consistency\n\n1. Good Solution : Use distributed cache alongside our search service\n- Store most recent results for a given search query, whenever queried search cache first, if not present hit index db, and then populate cache and return result to user\n- Have TTL of < 1 minute to guarantee we're never serving results that might not contain newly created posts.\n\n\n2. Great Solution : Use a CDN at the edge\n- Use CloudFare or AWS CloudFront to serve search results quickly\n- Most CDNs operate like a big set of geographically-spread HTTP caches.\n- configure an \"origin\" or target for the cache and configure DNS to route through the CDN.\n- If CDN doesn't get a hit, it proxies the request back to our servers to handle\n- add cache-control headers which tell our CDN when and for how long to cache a result.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "u3cAkY-fLSV_QY1AT0Bme",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -66409.7364741281,
      "y": -8727.334350334268,
      "width": 1696.958984375,
      "height": 825,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aA",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1515977315,
      "version": 2439,
      "versionNonce": 69999373,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784701003427,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to handle multi-keyword, phrase queries?\n- Single keyword searches are fine say \"taylor\" but what if search is \"taylor swift\" or even a longer keyword\n- Many search engines support an entire boolean language for specifying queries like \"+Taylor +Swift -Red\" but don't need them here\n\n\n1. Good Solution : Intersection and Filter\n- Request all postIds from \"Taylor\" and \"Swift\" individually\n- Intersect them i.e. find all posts which are in both \"Taylor\" and \"Swift\"\n- Grab the post content for each of these postIds and ensure they actually contain \"Taylor Swift\" and not a disconnecting string like \"My friend Taylor made a Swift exit\"\n- Return the posts that passed these filters in order.\n\nProblem : PostIds for individual keywords will be very large, making it difficult to pass over network and then intersect\nIf millions of posts for each keyword, we could be transferring MBs of data to be put to hashtable and intersected\nHard to achieve with our SLA or < 500ms\nAnd in worst case keywords match but they don't appear next to each other as a phrase, we may be filtering tons of results it will be computationally intensive.\n\n\n2. Great Solution : Bigrams or Shingles\n- Improve read-time efficiency by indexing \"bigrams\" or \"shingles\" (Lucene slang)\n- So the idea is for \"I saw Taylor Swift at the concert\" we create tokens for each pair of words:\n    - \"I saw\"\n    - \"saw Taylor\"\n    - \"Taylor Swift\"\n    - \"Swift at\"\n    - \"at the\"\n    - \"the concert\"\n- Insert these to our Likes and Creation indexes, when searching for \"Taylor Swift\" instead of grabbing them individually directly look for it and return.\n\nProblems : It dramatically increases the size of our indexes, problem is bigrams are far more unique so \"Swift at\" is far likely to occur in other posts making the index sparse.\nSo instead of 10Mil single-word keyword we end up with 100M indexed keys\nSolution is to extract bigrams that are likely to be searched and fallback to the intersection approach when there's no entry in the search index\nUse count-min sketch(CMS) or other probabilistic structures to determine frequency of bigrams occurrence across indexed posts\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to handle multi-keyword, phrase queries?\n- Single keyword searches are fine say \"taylor\" but what if search is \"taylor swift\" or even a longer keyword\n- Many search engines support an entire boolean language for specifying queries like \"+Taylor +Swift -Red\" but don't need them here\n\n\n1. Good Solution : Intersection and Filter\n- Request all postIds from \"Taylor\" and \"Swift\" individually\n- Intersect them i.e. find all posts which are in both \"Taylor\" and \"Swift\"\n- Grab the post content for each of these postIds and ensure they actually contain \"Taylor Swift\" and not a disconnecting string like \"My friend Taylor made a Swift exit\"\n- Return the posts that passed these filters in order.\n\nProblem : PostIds for individual keywords will be very large, making it difficult to pass over network and then intersect\nIf millions of posts for each keyword, we could be transferring MBs of data to be put to hashtable and intersected\nHard to achieve with our SLA or < 500ms\nAnd in worst case keywords match but they don't appear next to each other as a phrase, we may be filtering tons of results it will be computationally intensive.\n\n\n2. Great Solution : Bigrams or Shingles\n- Improve read-time efficiency by indexing \"bigrams\" or \"shingles\" (Lucene slang)\n- So the idea is for \"I saw Taylor Swift at the concert\" we create tokens for each pair of words:\n    - \"I saw\"\n    - \"saw Taylor\"\n    - \"Taylor Swift\"\n    - \"Swift at\"\n    - \"at the\"\n    - \"the concert\"\n- Insert these to our Likes and Creation indexes, when searching for \"Taylor Swift\" instead of grabbing them individually directly look for it and return.\n\nProblems : It dramatically increases the size of our indexes, problem is bigrams are far more unique so \"Swift at\" is far likely to occur in other posts making the index sparse.\nSo instead of 10Mil single-word keyword we end up with 100M indexed keys\nSolution is to extract bigrams that are likely to be searched and fallback to the intersection approach when there's no entry in the search index\nUse count-min sketch(CMS) or other probabilistic structures to determine frequency of bigrams occurrence across indexed posts\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
