      "versionNonce": 1493245421,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784703535524,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to address large volumes of writes?\n- We have very high write volume for post creation and likes, and likes volume being >> posts volume\n\nPost Creation\n- When post created call the ingestion service to tokenize the post and writes the postId to Creation and Likes indexes\n- if 100 words in a post, we trigger 100+ writes, and a lot of posts are created simultaneously\n- Introduce Kafka and fan out the creation requests to multiple ingestion instances and partition the load\n- This Buffers request so that we can handle bursts of post creation. Scale our index by sharding the indexes by keywords.\n- This way writes are spread across many Redis instances.\n\n\nLike Event\n- Like is far more frequent updating the index for each like is not efficient\n\n1. Good Solution : Batch likes before writing to DB\n- Instead for every like update batch for say 30s, then instead of needing to write 500 times for a viral post we make 1 update and increment score by 500\n- Introduce a batcher service which accepts events and aggregates them, and pushes the aggregated event to kafka and then consumed by Ingestion Service.\nProblem : This doesn't solve our problem for large number of writes as most of the posts are not viral, if a post receives 1 like per min uniformly, then batching is useless and we end up with same number of likes\n\n\n2. Great Solution : Two stage Architecture\n- Only update like count in our indexes when it passes a specific milestone like powers of 2 or 10\n- So we would write to index when post has a 1 like, 2 likes, 4 likes etc. reducing the number of writes exponentially, so we make 10 writes for 1000likes instead of 1000\n- But this means our index is inherently stale, the data cannot be completely trusted but the ordering is approx correct, the post with 10k likes will definitely be in front of 0 or 1 like\n- If we want to retrieve N posts, we grab the top N^2 posts from like index, for each post we query like service for up-to date like count and sort based on that fresh like count before returning\n- This way the storage is approximate but the end result is precise. \n- This is very common practice in production search engines\nProblems : complex to implement",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to address large volumes of writes?\n- We have very high write volume for post creation and likes, and likes volume being >> posts volume\n\nPost Creation\n- When post created call the ingestion service to tokenize the post and writes the postId to Creation and Likes indexes\n- if 100 words in a post, we trigger 100+ writes, and a lot of posts are created simultaneously\n- Introduce Kafka and fan out the creation requests to multiple ingestion instances and partition the load\n- This Buffers request so that we can handle bursts of post creation. Scale our index by sharding the indexes by keywords.\n- This way writes are spread across many Redis instances.\n\n\nLike Event\n- Like is far more frequent updating the index for each like is not efficient\n\n1. Good Solution : Batch likes before writing to DB\n- Instead for every like update batch for say 30s, then instead of needing to write 500 times for a viral post we make 1 update and increment score by 500\n- Introduce a batcher service which accepts events and aggregates them, and pushes the aggregated event to kafka and then consumed by Ingestion Service.\nProblem : This doesn't solve our problem for large number of writes as most of the posts are not viral, if a post receives 1 like per min uniformly, then batching is useless and we end up with same number of likes\n\n\n2. Great Solution : Two stage Architecture\n- Only update like count in our indexes when it passes a specific milestone like powers of 2 or 10\n- So we would write to index when post has a 1 like, 2 likes, 4 likes etc. reducing the number of writes exponentially, so we make 10 writes for 1000likes instead of 1000\n- But this means our index is inherently stale, the data cannot be completely trusted but the ordering is approx correct, the post with 10k likes will definitely be in front of 0 or 1 like\n- If we want to retrieve N posts, we grab the top N^2 posts from like index, for each post we query like service for up-to date like count and sort based on that fresh like count before returning\n- This way the storage is approximate but the end result is precise. \n- This is very common practice in production search engines\nProblems : complex to implement",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
